> = Private Property and Things =
> 
> 041117, JunkerIn: Started. And continued.
> 
> First, consider the things to which the ideas of private property are applied:
> 
> :Chattels (movables)
> :Land (immovables)
> :Animals
> :Humans
> 
> The only category that is not characterized by controversy is that of chattels, and even there many have their doubts. Much of the controversy comes from the area of law. Unfortunately, most legal discussion is erroneous because of the undue influence stemming from government, an influence that has been on-going for centuries. There "history and precedent" and more "history and precedent" are the key concepts rather than clear thought. That is also the effect of government as government has been the major writer of "history and precedent" and that always in its own interest rather than in the interest of humans. Another major source of legal theory, consideration, and writing is the university system which has been vying for government money and approval for centuries as well. And that of course makes it harder to convincingly argue against the majority of current legal stands on property.
> 
> Land. There is argument that land is a separate category of thing because it cannot be moved and thus should only be subject to temporary 'ownership' dues to direct occupation and utilization.
> 
> Animals. Animals rights groups argue that cruelty to animals and a host of other emotionally based arguments calls for the non-ownability of animals.
> 
> Humans. Owned in the US until 1865 and still done in parts of the world shows that the idea is current and variant.
> 
> Chattels. Given that the other three are debated, why not chattels too. 
> 
> Thus, each area has it own set of arguments. The general ancap view remains at all categories being permanently ownable and heritable except humans which are not ownable (and thus as well heritable).
> 
> 
> 
> 
> PrivatePropertyAndThingsTalk
³1revision³17³1tscreate³11095176923³1text_default³1ip³2217.247.143.218³2ts³21100690157³2version³21³2name³2text_default³2data³2summary³3+more text³3text³3= Private Property and Things =

041117, JunkerIn: Started. And continued.

First, consider the things to which the ideas of private property are applied:

:Chattels (movables)
:Land (immovables)
:Animals
:Humans

The only category that is not characterized by controversy is that of chattels, and even there many have their doubts. Much of the controversy comes from the area of law. Unfortunately, most legal discussion is erroneous because of the undue influence stemming from government, an influence that has been on-going for centuries. There "history and precedent" and more "history and precedent" are the key concepts rather than clear thought. That is also the effect of government as government has been the major writer of "history and precedent" and that always in its own interest rather than in the interest of humans. Another major source of legal theory, consideration, and writing is the university system which has been vying for government money and approval for centuries as well. And that of course makes it harder to convincingly argue against the majority of current legal stands on property.

Land. There is argument that land is a separate category of thing because it cannot be moved and thus should only be subject to temporary 'ownership' dues to direct occupation and utilization.

Animals. Animals rights groups argue that cruelty to animals and a host of other emotionally based arguments calls for the non-ownability of animals.

Humans. Owned in the US until 1865 and still done in parts of the world shows that the idea is current and variant.

Chattels. Given that the other three are debated, why not chattels too. 

Thus, each area has it own set of arguments. The general ancap view remains at all categories being permanently ownable and heritable except humans which are not ownable (and thus as well heritable).




PrivatePropertyAndThingsTalk
